# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent
# reviewable — the settings page has no reviewer, and nothing in the
# repository said what the gate was supposed to be (antimatter-studios/diskjockey#197).
#
# The two names below are what ci.yml produces on a pull request today, and
# they are already what protection requires, so this is a pin rather than a
# correction. It is still worth landing. The `test` job is a matrix —
# `name: test-${{ matrix.os }}` over `os: [ubuntu-latest]` — and the day a
# second os joins that list it produces a check nobody required, which is the
# exact shape of fake coverage the audit found elsewhere in the constellation:
# a leg that runs, goes red, and merges anyway. With the list declared here,
# adding a leg to the matrix without adding it here is a visible omission in
# the same diff rather than an invisible one on a settings page.
#
# `validate against kernel btrfs driver` is the kernel-gate job, and it is the
# one that decides whether this driver reads Btrfs: real filesystems built by
# mkfs.btrfs, described by btrfs-progs and mounted by the in-kernel driver.
# The unit suite cannot replace it — it compares the parser against fixtures
# built from the same reading of the spec, so a misread field is consistent
# with itself and passes. It is required, not advisory.
#
# release.yml is deliberately absent: it triggers on a version tag, never on a
# pull request, and a required check that never reports blocks every merge
# forever.
#
# Names must match the check-run names GitHub reports. These are the job
# `name:` values, not the job ids, and the matrix leg is spelled
# `test-ubuntu-latest` (this workflow interpolates the os into the name
# itself, so there is no `test / ubuntu-latest` job-matrix spelling here).
# Verified against both main's check-runs and the latest pull_request
# check-suite on 2026-09-17: run 35242813679 on 28f8ee6 produced exactly these
# two, both success, and check-suite 95446651348 (PR branch
# feat/62-cross-subvolume) produced the same two and nothing else.
[checks]
	required = test-ubuntu-latest
	required = validate against kernel btrfs driver
